ce is unique (no free variables), return it\n5. If free variables exist:\n   a. If p^f ≤ threshold (e.g., 10^7), enumerate all\n   b. Otherwise, try meet-in-the-middle on free variables\n\nFor the meet-in-the-middle on free variables in the counter puzzle:\n- Split free variables into two groups\n- For group 1: enumerate p^{f/2} assignments, compute the resulting solution vector (length M) and cost sum\n- Store in a map from vector to (best_cost, assignment_config)\n- For group 2: enumerate p^{f/2} assignments, compute solution vector, combine with group 1\n- The combination is: x = x_1 + x_2 (element-wise, mod p)\n- cost = sum_i (x_1_i + x_2_i - p * floor((x_1_i + x_2_i) / p))\n\nWait, for combining in the counter puzzle meet-in-the-middle:\n- Group 1 produces a partial solution x_1 (mod p) with cost_1 = sum_i x_1_i\n- Group 2 produces a partial solution x_2 (mod p) with cost_2 = sum_i x_2_i\n- Total: x = (x_1 + x_2) mod p\n- Total cost: sum_i ((x_1_i + x_2_i) mod p)\n\nThis is NOT cost_1 + cost_2 because of the modulo wrap.\n\nFor meet-in-the-middle to work, we need to be able to combine efficiently. If we store x_1 values and look up by required x_1 (which is (target - x_2) mod p), then:\n- For each group 2 assignment, compute x_2\n- Look up x_1 = (x_p + ...) ... hmm, this still doesn't work because any pair (x_1, x_2) gives a valid solution.\n\nWait, for the counter puzzle, ALL combinations of null space vectors produce a valid solution. So we're not looking up by \"required x_1\". Instead, for each group 2 assignment, we'd need to find the best group 1 assignment to combine with, and the best one depends on the cost (which is nonlinear).\n\nThis doesn't work with simple hash lookup.\n\nWhat if we enumerate group 1 and store (vector, cost, config) in an array, and for group 2, iterate through the array to find the best match? That's O(p^{f/2} * p^{f/2}) = O(p^f), which is just brute force.\n\nOK so meet-in-the-middle doesn't directly work for the counter puzzle because the cost is not additive.\n\nFor it to work, we'd need:\ncost(x_1 + x_2) = cost(x_1) + cost(x_2) - something\n\nThe \"something\" is: p * |{i: x_1_i + x_2_i ≥ p}|\n\nSo:\ntotal_cost = cost_1 + cost_2 - p * (number of wraparounds)\n\nThe number of wraparounds is: sum_i [x_1_i + x_2_i ≥ p]\n\nSo for each pair (x_1, x_2), the cost is:\ntotal = cost_1 + cost_2 - p * overlaps\n\nwhere overlaps = count of positions where x_1_i + x_2_i ≥ p.\n\nThis still depends on both x_1 and x_2, not just their individual costs.\n\nBut if we fix x_2, then:\ntotal_cost(x_1, x_2) = cost_1(x_1) + cost_2(x_2) - p * count_i (x_1_i + x_2_i ≥ p)\n\nFor a given x_2, we want to find x_1 with the best combined cost. This requires knowing the count of wraparounds, which depends on the actual vector values.\n\nFor meet-in-the-middle to work, we could store for x_1 not just the total cost but the actual vector entries. Then when matching with x_2, we compute the combined cost.\n\nThis means: for each group 2 assignment, we iterate through all group 1 assignments. That's O(p^{f/2} * p^{f/2}) = O(p^f), which is brute force.\n\nHmm, what if the number of distinct x_1 vectors is much smaller than p^{f_1} due to collisions? The vectors are in Z_p^M, and the number of possible vectors is p^M. For M = 40 and p = 97, p^M is enormous, so collisions are rare. Most assignments produce distinct vectors.\n\nOK I think I need to accept that for the counter puzzle, meet-in-the-middle on the free variables doesn't give a speedup because the cost function is not additive.\n\nSo for the counter puzzle, my algorithm will be:\n1. Gaussian elimination to find the solution space\n2. Enumeration of free variables when feasible\n3. For difficult cases, fall back to a smarter approach\n\nFor the \"smarter approach\", I think the answer is to use meet-in-the-middle on the ORIGINAL variables (not the free variables), using the effect vectors as the stored values.\n\nWait, I just realized: for the counter puzzle, the m