d solution.\n\nLet me think formally. For two sorted arrays L1 < L2 (lexicographically) and a sorted array R (disjoint index sets), is merge(L1, R) always lexicographically ≤ merge(L2, R)?\n\nThe merge algorithm processes elements in order. The first element of merge(L1, R) is either L1[0] or R[0]. Similarly for merge(L2, R), it's L2[0] or R[0].\n\nIf L1[0] <= R[0] and L2[0] <= R[0]: first element is min(L1[0], L2[0]). Since L1 < L2 lexicographically, L1[0] <= L2[0], so merge(L1, R)[0] = L1[0] <= L2[0] = merge(L2, R)[0]. So first element of merge L1 is ≤ first element of merge L2.\n\nIf L1[0] > R[0] and L2[0] > R[0]: both have R[0] as first element, which is the same.\n\nIf L1[0] <= R[0] < L2[0]: merge(L1, R)[0] = L1[0] <= R[0] < L2[0] = merge(L2, R)[0]. So L1 gives smaller first element.\n\nIf L2[0] <= R[0] < L1[0]: but this can't happen because L1 < L2 lexicographically implies L1[0] <= L2[0], and L2[0] <= R[0] implies L1[0] <= L2[0] <= R[0], so L1[0] <= R[0], so this case doesn't occur.\n\nOK so in ALL cases, the first element of merge(L1, R) is ≤ the first element of merge(L2, R). If they're different, we're done.\n\nIf the first elements are equal, then we need to look at the second element. Zig's merge process for the first element: if both have R[0] as first element, we pop R[0] from R and continue. If both have L[0] as first element and L1[0] = L2[0], we pop that common element and continue.\n\nIn general, merge(L1, R) and merge(L2, R) will have the same first k-1 elements and differ at the k-th element. The difference will be at a position where one has an element from L that's smaller than the other's.\n\nSince L1 < L2 lexicographically, there's a first position where L1 differs from L2. The merge will encounter this difference at the same or later position. At the first position where merge(L1, R) and merge(L2, R) differ, it will be because either:\na. One has an element from L and the other from R at that position → but since both start with the same merged prefix, the R elements consumed are the same, so the next R element is the same. The L elements consumed are the same up to the point of divergence.\nb. Both have elements from L, and the first differing L elements are encountered → since L1's differing element is smaller, merge(L1, R) has a smaller element.\n\nI think it IS true that if L1 < L2 lexicographically, then merge(L1, R) ≤ merge(L2, R) lexicographically (for disjoint sorted arrays). This means I only need to store the best left solution for each mask.\n\nPROOF:\nLet L1, L2 be sorted arrays with distinct elements (button indices are unique).\nLet R be a sorted array with elements distinct from both L1 and L2.\nL1 < L2 lexicographically.\n\nLet's consider the merge process. Since indices are unique, the merge is straightforward: we iterate through elements in sorted order.\n\nLet the multiset S1 = set(L1) ∪ set(R) and S2 = set(L2) ∪ set(R).\nLet the sorted arrays be M1 = sorted(S1) and M2 = sorted(S2).\n\nSince set(R) is the same for both, and L1 ≠ L2:\nLet x be the first element that differs between S1 and S2. This x must be in one of L1, L2 and not the other (since R is the same).\n\nSince L1 < L2 lexicographically, let p be the first position where L1 and L2 differ. At position p, L1[p] < L2[p].\n\nNow, in the combined sets, up to position p in the sorted order of L1 and L2, the same elements have been consumed. At position p, L1 contributes L1[p] and L2 contributes L2[p].\n\nIn M1, L1[p] appears at some position (possibly after some elements of R that are < L1[p]).\nIn M2, L2[p] appears at some position (possibly after some elements of R that are < L2[p]).\n\nIf there are elements of R between L1[p] and L2[p], then M1 would have those R elements after L1[p], while M2 would have L2[p] before those R elements. The first differing element between M1 and M2 would be L1[p] vs L2[p], and L1[p] < L2[p], so M1 < M2.\n\nOtherwise, if there are no R elements between L1[p] and L2[p], then at the position where