eft group: indices 0 to mid-1\n  const leftSize = mid;\n  const rightSize = M - mid;\n  \n  // Store left results: mask -> { count, array }\n  const leftMap = new Map<bigint, { count: number; arr: number[] }>();\n  \n  // Enumerate left subsets\n  for (let subset = 0; subset < (1 << leftSize); subset++) {\n    let mask = 0n;\n    let count = 0;\n    const arr: number[] = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < leftSize; i++) {\n      if (subset & (1 << i)) {\n        mask ^= buttonMasks[i];\n        count++;\n        arr.push(i);\n      }\n    }\n    \n    const existing = leftMap.get(mask);\n    if (!existing || count < existing.count || (count === existing.count && lexicographicallySmaller(arr, existing.arr))) {\n      leftMap.set(mask, { count, arr });\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Enumerate right subsets and find best\n  let bestCount = Infinity;\n  let bestArr: number[] | null = null;\n  \n  for (let subset = 0; subset < (1 << rightSize); subset++) {\n    let mask = 0n;\n    let count = 0;\n    const arr: number[] = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < rightSize; i++) {\n      if (subset & (1 << i)) {\n        mask ^= buttonMasks[mid + i];\n        count++;\n        arr.push(mid + i);\n      }\n    }\n    \n    const needed = targetMask ^ mask;\n    const left = leftMap.get(needed);\n    if (left) {\n      const totalCount = left.count + count;\n      const combinedArr = mergeSorted(left.arr, arr);\n      \n      if (totalCount < bestCount || (totalCount === bestCount && lexicographicallySmaller(combinedArr, bestArr))) {\n        bestCount = totalCount;\n        bestArr = combinedArr;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return bestArr;\n}\n```\n\nWait, lexicographicallySmaller comparison needs to handle null bestArr.\n\nLet me also think about the mergeSorted function: it takes two sorted arrays and returns a merged sorted array.\n\n```typescript\nfunction mergeSorted(a: number[], b: number[]): number[] {\n  const result: number[] = [];\n  let i = 0, j = 0;\n  while (i < a.length && j < b.length) {\n    if (a[i] < b[j]) result.push(a[i++]);\n    else result.push(b[j++]);\n  }\n  while (i < a.length) result.push(a[i++]);\n  while (j < b.length) result.push(b[j++]);\n  return result;\n}\n\nfunction lexicographicallySmaller(a: number[], b: number[] | null): boolean {\n  if (b === null) return true;\n  for (let i = 0; i < Math.min(a.length, b.length); i++) {\n    if (a[i] !== b[i]) return a[i] < b[i];\n  }\n  return a.length < b.length;\n}\n```\n\nThis should work. But I need to be careful about BigInt bitmasks going beyond 32 bits. With up to 40 lights, I need BigInt with up to 40 bits. BigInt in JavaScript can handle this easily.\n\nWait, actually, I can also use two 32-bit numbers to avoid BigInt overhead. But BigInt is simpler and should be fast enough for 2^20 ≈ 1M entries.\n\nActually, for performance, I could use a string key or a number (if lights ≤ 32). But BigInt is cleaner.\n\nHmm, but BigInt as a Map key works fine.\n\nLet me also think about whether storing 2^20 entries in a Map is feasible. That's about 1M entries, which should be OK for a JavaScript Map.\n\nNow, for the Counter Puzzle:\n\n### Counter Puzzle (Part 2) - Z_p\n\nThe approach:\n1. Augmented matrix [A | b] over Z_p\n2. Gaussian elimination to RREF\n3. Find particular solution and null space basis\n4. If no free variables, return the unique solution (handling mod values 0..p-1)\n5. If free variables exist, we need to minimize the L1 norm\n\nFor Gaussian elimination over Z_p:\n- Pivot finding: find a row with non-zero entry in the current column\n- If not found, move to next column\n- Scale row so pivot value = 1 (multiply by modular inverse)\n- Eliminate all other rows\n\nAfter Gaussian elimination:\n- Pivot columns correspond to basic variables\n- Non-pivot columns are free variables\n\nFor the solution space:\n- Particular solution: set free variables to 0, read off pivot variables from the RHS\n- Null space basis: for each free variable j, set it to 1 (and all others to 0), solve for pivots\n\nNow, for minimizing sum of x_i:\n\nLet x_p be the particular solution (size M)\nLet N be the M × f null space matrix (f = number of free variables)\n\nAll solutions: x = x_p + N*t mod p, where t ∈ Z_p^f\n\nCost: sum_i (x_i as integer 0..p-1)\n\nWe need min_{t ∈ Z_p^f} ||x_p + N*t||₁\n\nFor the optimization, I'll use:\n- If p^f is small (≤ 10^6), enumerate and find best\n- Otherwise, use meet-in-the-middle\n\nFor meet-in-the-middle on free variables:\n- Split f into two groups\n- For each group, enumerate all p^{group_size} assignments\n- For group 1, store the resulting (partial solution vector, cost, t) for each assignment\n- For group 2, for each assignment, compute v₂, combine with group 1\n\nBut as I discussed, the cost doesn't decompose additively. Let me think again...\n\nActually, for the meet-in-the-middle, here's what we do:\n\nSplit the free variables t into t₁ (size f₁) and t₂ (size f₂).\n\nFor group 1 (t₁), enumerate all p^{f₁} assignments. For each:\n- v₁ = N[:, 0:f₁] * t₁ (mod p) — the contribution to the M variables\n- cost contribution not directly defined since cost depends on the mod wrap\n\nHmm, the total cost = sum_i ((x_p_i + v₁_i + v₂_i) mod p). This can't be decomposed as cost₁ + cost₂.\n\nBUT, look what happens if we precompute:\n\nFor group 1: For each assignment t₁, compute v₁ (size M) and store it.\n\nFor group 2: For each assignment t₂, compute v₂ (size M). Then:\n- The combined solution is x = (x_p + v₁ + v₂) mod p\n- cost = sum_i x_i\n\nFor a given v₂, we want to find v₁ that minimizes cost = sum_i ((x_p_i + v₁_i + v₂_i) mod p).\n\nThis is: sum_i ((x_p_i + v₂_i + v₁_i) mod p)\n\nLet s_i = (x_p_i + v₂_i) mod p. Then cost = sum_i ((s_i + v₁_i) mod p).\n\nFor each i, (s_i + v₁_i) mod p = s_i + v₁_i if s_i + v₁_i < p, or s_i + v₁_i - p if s_i + v₁_i ≥ p.\n\nSo: cost = sum_i (s_i + v₁_i) - p * count_i(s_i + v₁_i ≥ p)\n       = sum_i s_i + sum_i v₁_i - p * wraps(v₁)\n\nWhere wraps(v₁) is the number of components where s_i + v₁_i ≥